WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.498
In the last topic we built unit cells.

2
00:00:03.498 --> 00:00:06.675
Now we count atoms along lines and on planes, and

3
00:00:06.675 --> 00:00:09.972
then we use X rays to actually see those planes.

4
00:00:13.092 --> 00:00:15.696
Linear density is the number of atoms per unit

5
00:00:15.696 --> 00:00:18.752
length whose centers lie on a direction vector.

6
00:00:19.172 --> 00:00:23.281
Your lecture's example is the one zero zero direction in F C C.

7
00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:28.917
Along a cube edge, only the two corner atoms are centered on the line, half each.

8
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:31.265
That is one atom over a length a.

9
00:00:31.685 --> 00:00:34.645
Compare the face diagonal, one one zero.

10
00:00:34.645 --> 00:00:38.765
Two corner halves plus the face atom: two atoms over four R.

11
00:00:38.765 --> 00:00:41.203
That is one over two R, higher.

12
00:00:41.203 --> 00:00:44.627
One one zero is the close packed direction in F C C.

13
00:00:48.327 --> 00:00:53.277
Planar density is the number of atoms per unit area centered on a plane.

14
00:00:53.277 --> 00:00:56.849
Your lecture uses the one one zero plane in F C C.

15
00:00:57.269 --> 00:01:01.322
The plane is a rectangle, four R long and two R root two high.

16
00:01:01.322 --> 00:01:03.765
Its area is eight R squared root two.

17
00:01:04.185 --> 00:01:06.529
Count only atoms centered on the plane.

18
00:01:06.529 --> 00:01:11.114
Four corners at a quarter each, and two face atoms on the edges at a half each.

19
00:01:11.114 --> 00:01:11.980
Two atoms.

20
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.474
So the planar density is two over eight R squared root

21
00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:18.776
two, which is one over four R squared root two.

22
00:01:22.477 --> 00:01:23.634
Why do we care?

23
00:01:23.634 --> 00:01:28.419
Properties depend on these densities, like the speed of sound along a direction.

24
00:01:28.419 --> 00:01:32.257
Most importantly, slip, the way metals deform plastically.

25
00:01:32.676 --> 00:01:36.270
Slip happens on the planes with the greatest density of atoms,

26
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.537
along the directions with the highest linear density.

27
00:01:39.537 --> 00:01:44.165
In short: the closest packed directions, on the closest packed planes.

28
00:01:47.965 --> 00:01:50.875
A single crystal is one continuous lattice.

29
00:01:50.875 --> 00:01:54.892
Some applications need them: nickel alloy turbine blades for high

30
00:01:54.892 --> 00:01:59.257
temperature strength, and diamond single crystals for abrasives.

31
00:01:59.677 --> 00:02:04.467
But most engineering materials are polycrystalline: many grains, each one a

32
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:08.853
single crystal, from about a nanometer to two centimeters across.

33
00:02:09.273 --> 00:02:14.304
In a single crystal, properties depend on direction: anisotropic.

34
00:02:14.304 --> 00:02:19.147
In B C C iron, the elastic modulus is one hundred twenty five gigapascals

35
00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:24.178
along one zero zero, but two hundred seventy three along one one one.

36
00:02:24.597 --> 00:02:29.819
In a polycrystal with randomly oriented grains, those differences average out.

37
00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:33.458
It is isotropic, about two hundred ten gigapascals.

38
00:02:33.458 --> 00:02:38.152
But if the grains are textured, the polycrystal is anisotropic again.

39
00:02:41.952 --> 00:02:45.800
How do we measure atomic planes? X ray diffraction.

40
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.292
X rays are electromagnetic waves with high energy and short

41
00:02:49.292 --> 00:02:53.081
wavelengths, comparable to the spacing between atoms.

42
00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:57.379
Diffraction happens when a wave meets a series of regularly spaced

43
00:02:57.379 --> 00:03:02.197
obstacles that scatter it, with a spacing comparable to the wavelength.

44
00:03:02.197 --> 00:03:04.548
Planes of atoms are exactly that.

45
00:03:04.548 --> 00:03:07.427
So X R D can identify unknown materials, find

46
00:03:07.427 --> 00:03:11.070
crystal structures, and measure lattice parameters.

47
00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:13.325
The scattered waves interfere.

48
00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:16.352
In phase, they add: constructive interference.

49
00:03:16.352 --> 00:03:20.073
Out of phase by half a wavelength, they cancel: destructive.

50
00:03:23.973 --> 00:03:27.815
Now the geometry. Two parallel X rays strike two neighboring

51
00:03:27.815 --> 00:03:31.312
planes, a distance d apart, at an angle theta.

52
00:03:31.733 --> 00:03:34.048
The lower ray travels farther.

53
00:03:34.048 --> 00:03:38.927
The extra path is S Q plus Q T, and each piece equals d sine theta.

54
00:03:39.347 --> 00:03:41.777
For constructive interference, that extra

55
00:03:41.777 --> 00:03:44.478
path must be a whole number of wavelengths.

56
00:03:44.478 --> 00:03:48.150
n lambda equals two d sine theta. That is Bragg's law.

57
00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.749
For cubic crystals, the spacing between h k l planes is a over

58
00:03:52.749 --> 00:03:56.928
the square root of h squared plus k squared plus l squared.

59
00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:01.753
In a diffractometer, the detector sweeps through angles two theta.

60
00:04:01.753 --> 00:04:05.870
A powder, or polycrystalline, sample guarantees that some grains

61
00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.359
are oriented so that every set of planes can diffract.

62
00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:12.504
Each material gives its own unique set of peaks.

63
00:04:16.203 --> 00:04:19.088
Your lecture's example: B C C iron.

64
00:04:19.088 --> 00:04:23.917
Find the spacing of the two two zero planes and the diffraction angle.

65
00:04:23.917 --> 00:04:27.617
a is zero point two eight six six nanometers, lambda is

66
00:04:27.617 --> 00:04:31.756
zero point one seven nine zero nanometers, first order.

67
00:04:32.177 --> 00:04:35.615
d two two zero is zero point two eight six six over the

68
00:04:35.615 --> 00:04:39.553
square root of four plus four plus zero, root eight.

69
00:04:39.553 --> 00:04:43.054
That is zero point one zero one three nanometers.

70
00:04:43.473 --> 00:04:48.332
Then sine theta equals lambda over two d: zero point one seven nine over

71
00:04:48.332 --> 00:04:53.318
zero point two zero two six, which is zero point eight eight three.

72
00:04:53.738 --> 00:04:56.916
So theta is about sixty two degrees, and the diffraction

73
00:04:56.916 --> 00:05:00.836
angle, two theta, is about one hundred twenty four degrees.

74
00:05:04.736 --> 00:05:08.208
One last idea. Some materials have more than one crystal

75
00:05:08.208 --> 00:05:13.112
structure, depending on temperature and pressure. That is polymorphism.

76
00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:15.978
In pure elements, it is called allotropy.

77
00:05:16.398 --> 00:05:18.959
Carbon can be graphite or diamond.

78
00:05:18.959 --> 00:05:24.642
And iron is B C C below nine hundred twelve degrees Celsius, and F C C above it.

79
00:05:28.542 --> 00:05:30.438
Here is the summary of topic four.

80
00:05:30.858 --> 00:05:34.948
Linear density counts atoms on a line, planar density on a plane.

81
00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:40.541
Slip uses the densest planes and directions, and single crystals are anisotropic.

82
00:05:40.961 --> 00:05:44.423
And Bragg's law, n lambda equals two d sine theta,

83
00:05:44.423 --> 00:05:47.467
turns diffraction peaks into plane spacings.

84
00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:51.228
Next, the defects that real crystals always contain.
